###["Here are three possible next steps to generate additional features to improve the downstream task prediction (whether the person receives credit):\n\n### Step 1: Compute credit_per_month\npython\n# credit_per_month\n# Feature description: This feature calculates the amount of credit allocated per month based on the duration of the loan.\n# Usefulness: This gives an estimate of monthly financial burden due to the loan, which may highlight whether the individual can manage repayments.\ndf['credit_per_month'] = df['credit_amount'] / df['duration']\nend\n\n### Step 2: Encode employment_duration_score\npython\n# employment_duration_score\n# Feature description: Map employment categories into ordinal scores based on duration of employment (e.g., longer employment translates to more financial stability).\n# Usefulness: Longer employment is typically seen as an indicator of financial stability and lower credit risk.\nemployment_mapping = {\n    'unemployed': 0,     # No stability\n    '<1': 1,             # Low stability\n    '1<=X<4': 2,         # Moderate stability\n    '4<=X<7': 3,         # High stability\n    '>=7': 4             # Highest stability\n}\ndf['employment_duration_score'] = df['employment'].map(employment_mapping)\nend\n\n### Step 3: Generate savings_score\npython\n# savings_score\n# Feature description: Convert the savings_status categories into an ordinal score based on the amount of savings (e.g., higher savings are deemed less risky).\n# Usefulness: Savings often indicate financial cushion in case of unexpected events, directly affecting creditworthiness.\nsavings_mapping = {\n    'no known savings': 0,   # No financial cushion\n    '<100': 1,               # Minimal savings\n    '100<=X<500': 2,         # Moderate savings\n    '500<=X<1000': 3,        # High savings\n    '>=1000': 4              # Very high savings\n}\ndf['savings_score'] = df['savings_status'].map(savings_mapping)\nend\n\n### Optionally Dropped Columns\nHere are the reasoning and code for dropping redundant or transformed columns:\n\npython\n# Drop employment since it's now represented as employment_duration_score\ndf.drop(columns=['employment'], inplace=True)\n\n# Drop savings_status since it's now represented as savings_score\ndf.drop(columns=['savings_status'], inplace=True)\nend\n\n### Final Thoughts:\n1. credit_per_month conveys monthly financial stress due to loan and is easy to interpret.\n2. employment_duration_score transforms qualitative employment data into a numeric scale aligned with financial stability.\n3. savings_score simplifies the evaluation of financial cushion using domain-driven encoding.\n4. Dropping redundant columns prevents data leakage and ensures less complexity for the model.", "### Step 1: Log transformation of credit_amount\npython\n# log_credit_amount\n# Apply log transformation to the credit_amount to reduce skewness and handle large value ranges in the credit_amount feature.\n# Usefulness: Helps normalize the distribution of credit_amount, especially since this feature has a large range (338 to 15,945). Many machine learning algorithms perform better with normalized data.\ndf['log_credit_amount'] = df['credit_amount'].apply(lambda x: np.log1p(x))\nend\n\n### Step 2: Interaction between credit_to_income_ratio and duration\npython\n# credit_burden_duration_interaction\n# Compute an interaction feature between credit_to_income_ratio and duration by multiplying the two.\n# Usefulness: Captures the combined effect of the borrower’s financial burden (credit_to_income_ratio) and the length of their commitment (duration), which may better indicate their risk profile.\ndf['credit_burden_duration_interaction'] = df['credit_to_income_ratio'] * df['duration']\nend\n\n### Step 3: One-hot encode personal_status\npython\n# personal_status_encoded\n# Perform one-hot encoding on personal_status to create binary columns for unique categories.\n# Usefulness: Converts categorical information into machine interpretable numeric form, which eliminates any implicit ordinal assumptions and helps capture the impact of gender and marital status on credit risk.\npersonal_status_dummies = pd.get_dummies(df['personal_status'], prefix='personal_status')\ndf = pd.concat([df, personal_status_dummies], axis=1)\nend\n\n### Dropped Column\npython\n# Dropping personal_status\n# The original personal_status column is removed because its information is now encoded in the one-hot encoded columns.\ndf.drop(columns=['personal_status'], inplace=True)\nend\n\n### Reasoning Behind Steps:\n1. log_credit_amount: Credit amount has a wide range, and taking the log reduces skewness, stabilizing variances and benefiting models sensitive to different scales.\n2. credit_burden_duration_interaction: Captures the compounded effect of financial strain (credit_to_income_ratio) and duration, allowing the model to weigh long vs. short-term financial commitments more effectively.\n3. personal_status_encoded: One-hot encoding ensures that all statuses are appropriately represented as independent features, aiding interpretability and compatibility with algorithms. Dropping the original column prevents redundancy.", "Here is a simple, clear, and intuitive solution based solely on insights from the dataset description and attributes.\n\n### Step 1: Compute credit_per_month\npython\n# credit_per_month\n# Feature description: This feature calculates the amount of credit allocated per month based on the loan's duration.\n# Usefulness: Highlights the monthly financial burden due to the loan, indicating the borrower's strain in managing repayments.\ndf['credit_per_month'] = df['credit_amount'] / df['duration']\nend\n\n### Step 2: Encode employment_duration_score\npython\n# employment_duration_score\n# Feature description: Convert employment categories into ordinal scores based on the length of employment (longer employment implies higher financial stability).\n# Usefulness: Employment duration is a strong indicator of financial stability and creditworthiness in real-world scenarios.\nemployment_mapping = {\n    'unemployed': 0,     # No stability\n    '<1': 1,             # Low stability\n    '1<=X<4': 2,         # Moderate stability\n    '4<=X<7': 3,         # High stability\n    '>=7': 4             # Highest stability\n}\ndf['employment_duration_score'] = df['employment'].map(employment_mapping)\nend\n\n### Step 3: Encode savings_score\npython\n# savings_score\n# Feature description: Convert the savings_status categories into numeric scores based on the amount of savings (higher savings indicate less financial risk).\n# Usefulness: Savings provide a financial cushion, which is critical for assessing creditworthiness, especially during financial emergencies.\nsavings_mapping = {\n    'no known savings': 0,   # No financial cushion\n    '<100': 1,               # Minimal savings\n    '100<=X<500': 2,         # Moderate savings\n    '500<=X<1000': 3,        # High savings\n    '>=1000': 4              # Very high savings\n}\ndf['savings_score'] = df['savings_status'].map(savings_mapping)\nend\n\n### Dropped Columns\nWe will drop the following columns because their information is now encoded in the newly created numeric features, ensuring no redundancy:\npython\n# Dropping employment since it's now represented as employment_duration_score\ndf.drop(columns=['employment'], inplace=True)\n\n# Dropping savings_status since it's now represented as savings_score\ndf.drop(columns=['savings_status'], inplace=True)\nend\n\n### Reasoning for Selected Steps:\n1. credit_per_month: Captures monthly financial strain, aiding in evaluating if the borrower is overburdened by their loan relative to their ability to repay within the specified duration. \n2. employment_duration_score: Converts employment categories into an interpretable numeric ranking tied to financial stability, which is critical in assessing creditworthiness.\n3. savings_score: Savings are a strong indicator of financial security. This feature simplifies the assessment of savings into a numeric format that aligns with real-world risk perception.\n\nOverall, these features provide a simplified and meaningful representation of credit risk factors, helping the model better understand and predict outcomes effectively."]###,
